Pages = {291-295},
Note = {34th Medical Informatics Europe Conference-MIE, Athens, GREECE, AUG
   25-29, 2024},
Abstract = {The rapid shift to digital healthcare, accelerated by the COVID-19
   pandemic, holds promise for sustainable healthcare delivery and climate
   change mitigation. This study evaluates the Alltid oppet application
   through the lens of usability and patient satisfaction and their
   correlation with the intention for continued use. A cross-sectional
   analysis of primary care patients revealed that alignment with user
   needs significantly predicts long-term adoption, while frustration
   during use discourages it. Ease of use did not significantly affect the
   intention to continue, suggesting that usability alone is insufficient
   to drive sustained engagement. These findings highlight user-centred
   design's importance in digital healthcare solutions, suggesting that
   enduring adoption hinges on value perception and reducing user
   frustration. As the healthcare sector navigates its digital future, this
   study offers crucial insights into the design and implementation
   strategies that could underpin the environmental sustainability and
   resilience of healthcare systems.},
