Note = {Summer School on Modulation of Neuronal Signaling, Nida, ITALY, JUL
   12-21, 2000},
Organization = {NATO},
Abstract = {Neural signals can be subject to modulation from internal and external
   sources. This chapter is concerned with the latter, specifically the
   adaptation of photoreceptors to changes in ambient illumination.
   Vertebrate rods and cones sense and transmit light signals from the
   environment and have evolved highly sophisticated mechanisms for the
   amplification of photon energies into a neural response. In the process
   contrast constancy is maintained. This ensures the perception of a
   stable visual environment under large changes of illumination. High
   discrimination of contrast is ensured by the photoreceptor operating
   window shifting over the range of physiological light intensities as the
   background changes. During the transition from very low to high
   illumination the responses change from an optimum, slow mode for photon
   capture to a rapid mode. The psychophysical, physiological and
   biochemical properties are reviewed from a common perspective. Some new
   data are presented on photoreceptor kinetics and some unsolved problems
   are examined.},
