Note = {3rd National Conference on Injury Prevention and Control, BRISBANE,
   AUSTRALIA, MAY 09-12, 1999},
Organization = {Australian Injury Prevent Network; Conrod; Carrs; Qut; Univ Queensland;
   Queensland Inst Med Res; WAIC; Hlth \& Aged Care},
Abstract = {Education and reskilling is often used as a rehabilitation strategy for
   those with a permanent disability following accidents and trauma. For
   people with acquired brain injury, education can be particularly
   challenging and frustrating. Often the intellect remains intact but
   skills such as memory, processing and perception may be diminished or
   lost. As work continues on greater access for all students to tertiary
   education, this particular rapidly growing group is often overlooked.
   This paper reports on an ongoing project considering the use of computer
   and communications technology to address some of the cognitive deficits
   that hinder learning. The literature and work to date have highlighted
   some key issues for training materials in the tertiary sector. This
   paper outlines some of the special requirements of the student group
   with cognitive impairment, with examples of the results of the
   development using the systems are provided and some preliminary results
   of user testing/evaluation.},
