Note = {22nd Congress of the International Ergonomics Association-IEA, SOUTH
   KOREA, AUG 25-29, 2024},
Abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) systems are reshaping users' interactions
   with products and bringing new challenges for Human-Computer Interaction
   (HCI). Such a scenario highlights the need to apply a fundamental
   principle for human-centered design: transparency. However,
   ``transparency{''} is still a blurry concept in the context of AI
   systems due to the wide availability of definitions and analog terms
   across the literature of varied fields. Therefore, this paper aims to
   outline the concept of transparency for AI systems through the
   perspective of the HCI field by highlighting differences and
   similarities across varied definitions. We showed that transparency
   might be considered both as a driver for users' perceptions and other
   system characteristics or as an outcome of developmental techniques that
   aim to make systems more explainable. Whereas this work does not intend
   to provide a new definition for transparency, we expect our discussion
   to support further explorations on this concept.},
