Pages = {81-88},
Month = {FEB 15},
Note = {9th International Congress on Mental Dysfunction and Other Non-Motor
   Features in Parkinson's Disease and Related Disorders, Dresden, GERMANY,
   OCT 16-19, 2008},
Abstract = {Parkinson's disease (PD) is considered a multisystem disorder involving
   dopaminergic, noradrenergic. serotoninergic. and cholinergic systems,
   characterized by motor and non-motor symptoms. The causes of the
   non-motor symptoms in PD are multifactorial and unlikely to be explained
   by single lesions However, several evidence link them to damage of
   specific brainstem nuclei Numerous brainstem nuclei are engaged in
   fundamental homeostatic mechanisms, including gastrointestinal
   regulation, pain perception, mood control, and sleep-wake cycles In
   addition, these nuclei are locally interconnected in a complex manner
   and are subject to supraspinal control. The objective of this review is
   to provide a better overview of the current knowledge about the
   consequences of the involvement of specific brainstem nuclei to the most
   prevalent non-motor symptoms occurring in PD The multidisciplinary
   efforts of research directed to these non-nigral brainstem nuclei, in
   addition to the topographical and chronological spread of the disease -
   especially in the prodromal stages of PD. are discussed (C) 2009
   Elsevier B V. All rights reserved},
